After installing the Neovim plugin, run `:RivetInstallCLI` (or `:Rivet install-cli`) once to link its launcher into `~/.local/bin`. The link follows plugin updates and existing commands are never overwritten. If needed, add `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"` to your shell configuration and open a new terminal. For a different destination, use `:lua require('rivet').install_cli('/your/bin')`.

It requires Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT on macOS/Linux. To select LuaJIT, set `export RIVET_LUA=luajit` in your shell configuration. Removing or moving the plugin breaks the link; remove the old symlink and rerun the install command after moving it. To uninstall the CLI link, use `rm ~/.local/bin/rivet`.

You can also run the CLI from a [source checkout or extracted archive](/docs/start-here/#local-development) with `bin/rivet`, or add `bin/` to `PATH`.

## Terminal appearance and shell completions

The CLI uses Rivet’s maroon Catppuccin accents throughout help, previews, prompts,
diagnostics, and task summaries. `rivet --help` shows a grouped overview;
`rivet new --help` or `rivet help new` shows a command’s options and notes.
Color is automatic in terminals and disabled for redirected output, `TERM=dumb`,
or `NO_COLOR`. Set `RIVET_THEME=light` for a light terminal; the default is `dark`.
`RIVET_COLOR=always|never|auto` overrides detection (`NO_COLOR` still wins).
Generated file contents, completion scripts, and child-process output stay verbatim.

Completions cover commands, command-specific flags, fixed option values, and paths.
They do not run builds, query project tools, or download dependencies. Project target
and custom preset names are entered manually. Regenerate saved scripts after updating Rivet.

**Fish** — install in the user completion directory:

```fish
mkdir -p ~/.config/fish/completions
rivet completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/rivet.fish
source ~/.config/fish/completions/rivet.fish
```

If `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is set, use `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fish/completions` instead.
For just the current session: `rivet completions fish | source`.

**Bash** — add this to `~/.bashrc` (or `~/.bash_profile` on macOS):

```bash
source <(rivet completions bash)
```

**Zsh** — add this to `~/.zshrc`, after your completion framework initializes:

```zsh
# If your framework does not already initialize completion:
autoload -Uz compinit && compinit
source <(rivet completions zsh)
```

Open a new shell after editing its configuration. Bash and Zsh startup snippets
load the current completion definitions on each shell start; no separate package is needed.

## Shared creation options

```sh
rivet new ./engine --language cpp --standard 26 --modules false --preview
rivet new ./analysis_demo --language python --preview
```

| Option | Values and behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `--language` | `cpp` (default), `c`, `python`, `rust`, `swift`, or `lua`; additional providers can be registered in the running Lua process |
| `--name` | Project identifier; otherwise derived from the destination folder. Explicit names must satisfy the chosen provider's identifier rules. |
| `--kind` | `app` (default) or `library`; Rust also accepts `workspace`; Lua also accepts `plugin` |
| `--license` | `none` (default), `MIT`, `Apache-2.0`, `BSD-3-Clause`, or `GPL-3.0-only`; only for `new` |
| `--license-holder` | Copyright holder; required when selecting a license |
| `--license-year` | Four-digit year; defaults to the current year |
| `--git` | Separate `true` or `false`; defaults to `false`. Only for `new`. |
| `--interactive` | Terminal questionnaire with a final creation confirmation |
| `--preview` | Print the complete plan without applying it |
| `--build` | Run the language's Build workflow after creation; only for `new` |
| `--preset` | Initial build choice: `debug` (default) or `release` |

`rivet new . --git true --preview` plans creation in the current folder, with no nested folder. Names derived from folders are normalized to the provider's identifier rules; `--name` overrides the suggestion.

Git initialization follows file writes and respects Git's configured default branch. Rivet reuses an enclosing repository/worktree and does not stage, commit, or add a remote. Missing Git fails before writes; a later initialization failure leaves the created files and reports how to retry.

Bundled providers generate `.gitignore` even without Git initialization. Existing rules remain after generated rules; other file collisions are refused.

`--preview --build` only previews. Failed builds leave generated files available for correction. Python Build checks syntax; run Configure explicitly for environment setup, including uv sync.

## C++ creation options

These options apply to `--language cpp`:

| Option | Values and default |
| --- | --- |
| `--standard` | `17`, `20`, `23`, or `26` (default) |
| `--modules` | `true` or `false`; defaults to `false` in C++17 and `true` in later modes |
| `--module-layout` | `single` or `split` (default) |
| `--std-modules` | `true` or `false` (default); requires C++23+ and a compatible toolchain |
| `--profile` | `general` (default), `cpu`, `openmp`, `mpi`, `cuda`, `metal`, `webgpu`, `vulkan`, `opengl` |
| `--cuda-standard` | CUDA-only `17`, `20` (default), or `23`; independent of the host C++ mode |
| `--cuda-architectures` | CUDA-only `native` (default), `all`, `all-major`, or a quoted list such as `'80;90'`; numbers can have `-real` or `-virtual` suffixes |
| `--layout` | `feature` (default), `library`, or `flat` |
| `--style` | `LLVM` (default), `Google`, `Chromium`, `Mozilla`, `WebKit`, `Microsoft`, or `GNU` |
| `--header-guard` | `pragma` (default) or `ifndef` |
| `--generator` | `Ninja` (default) or quoted `"Unix Makefiles"`; Make requires both module options off |
| `--compiler` | Executable C++ compiler path, stored in local user presets |

Boolean options require separate, lowercase words: `--modules false`, not `--modules=false`, `--modules yes`, or a bare `--modules`. `--preview`, `--interactive`, and `--build` are switches without values. Use [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/) for module requirements and [HPC](/docs/hpc/) or [graphics](/docs/graphics/) for workload setup.

## Other language creation options

| Language | Options |
| --- | --- |
| C | `--standard 11`, `17` (default), or `23`; `--header-guard ifndef` (default) or `pragma`. CMake/Ninja starter; select the C compiler through local presets. |
| Python | `--manager python` (default) or `uv`; generated package requires Python 3.11+ |
| Rust | `--edition 2021` or `2024` (default); `--kind app`, `library`, or `workspace` |
| Swift | `--kind app` or `library`; generated SwiftPM manifest requires Swift tools 6.0+ |
| Lua | `--runtime lua` (default), `luajit`, or `neovim`; `--kind plugin` defaults to and requires `neovim` |

Use each language's native configuration for settings outside these creation options.

## Add files and targets

Target changes and build-registered file additions require `.rivet.json`. Run inside that project or add `--root PATH`. Generic file templates also work in external projects or ordinary folders, with `--root .` when no project marker is available:

```sh
rivet file Renderer --kind class --target engine_core --preview
rivet target viewer --kind app --preview
rivet target renderer --kind library --link-to engine --preview
```

| Provider | `file` behavior | `target` behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++ | `source`, `header`, `pair`, `class`, `module`, `module_pair`, `module_impl`, `c_source`, `c_header`, or `c_pair` | Application or static library; optional `--link-to` |
| C | Source, header, or pair in the chosen target | Application or static library |
| Python | A named module under the generated package | Add entry points directly in `pyproject.toml` |
| Rust | A named `.rs` file; declare it in the parent module yourself | Additional application under `src/bin/`; use Cargo for new libraries/workspace members |
| Swift | A source under a recorded target's conventional directory; preserves `Package.swift` | Application or library; refuses an externally edited generated package manifest |
| Lua | Module returning a table or runnable script | Reusable library or script executable |

For `module_impl`, provide `--module` with an existing module name. `module_pair` creates matching interface and implementation units. Lua's `embedded` template adds a separate host example with dependency/build changes; see [Lua](/docs/lua/). See [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) for path rules and [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/#source-and-target-ownership) for customized packages.

## Dependencies and file lifecycle

The C++ dependency command registers a pinned CPM dependency; other providers use their native dependency tools:

```sh
rivet dependency fmt --repository fmtlib/fmt --tag 11.2.0 \
  --target fmt::fmt --link-to engine_core --preview
```

`--version` aliases `--tag`; use an exact revision, not a version range. Scaffolding does not fetch it; a later configure can. See [CPM dependencies](/docs/dependencies/).

`move FROM TO` and `remove PATH` create plans for registered C/C++ sources or recorded foreign-language files; `module-rename FROM TO` applies to C++ modules. `repair MANAGED_FILE` backs up an edited managed file and restores its recorded snapshot. These commands, plus `new`, `file`, `target`, and `dependency`, accept `--preview`. Omit it to apply the CLI plan. Preview is not a dry-run mode for build tasks. See [Existing projects and file changes](/docs/existing-projects/) and [recovery](/docs/troubleshooting/#recover-an-edited-managed-file).

## Project discovery and tasks

Commands search upward for a Rivet manifest or supported native project. CMake discovery skips nested source-list fragments in favor of a parent project declaration or presets. `--root PATH` selects the root explicitly. [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/) can run tasks without `.rivet.json`.

| Provider | Available tasks and sequencing |
| --- | --- |
| C/C++ | Configure; Build configures first; Test and Run configure and build first; Format configures first |
| Python | Configure environment explicitly; Check/Build checks syntax; Test, Run, Format, Lint, and Package use selected native tools |
| Rust | Configure reads metadata; Check, Build, Test, Run, Format, Lint, and Package invoke Cargo |
| Swift | Configure reads metadata; Check/Build invokes SwiftPM build; Test, Run, Format, Lint; Xcode handoff on macOS |
| Lua | Configure reports runtime version; Check/Build check syntax; Test and Run execute scripts; Format/Lint use optional StyLua/Luacheck |

Workflows stop at the first failed step. Optional formatters and linters must be installed separately. Native tasks can resolve or download declared dependencies; uv Configure syncs its environment.

```sh
rivet build --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet test --root ./engine --preset release
rivet run --root ./engine --target viewer
rivet targets --root ./engine
```

For CMake, `--preset` selects a configure preset. `--build-preset` and `--test-preset` select associated native presets; a unique association is automatic. `--build-target` selects the Build target; `--target` selects Run's executable. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) for preset rules.

Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, and Lua use `debug` or `release` choices, not CMake preset files. Cargo binary selectors can use `package::binary`. Explicit runtime arguments, environments, working directories, and optional `benchmark`, `profile`, `submit`, `status`, and `cancel` hooks live in `.rivet.local.json`. See [Runtime options](/docs/task-options/).

## Inspect and configure

```sh
rivet doctor --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet cache --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet cache BUILD_TESTING OFF --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet styles
rivet layouts
rivet --help
```

Cache editing requires a CMake provider. Styles and layouts describe C++ creation choices. Doctor inspects tools and project settings without building or installing; errors produce a nonzero exit status, warnings do not. See [Doctor coverage](/docs/doctor/).

## Tool configuration and template upgrades

```sh
rivet config
rivet config clang-format
rivet config clang-format ColumnLimit 100 --preview
rivet config clangd CompileFlags.Add '["-Wall"]' --preview
rivet upgrade --preview
```

`config` lists tools; `config TOOL` lists common keys. Adding `KEY VALUE` previews or applies a validated edit. See [Configuration](/docs/configuration/).

`upgrade` previews or applies supplementary-template updates while preserving local edits. It does not download a plugin version; see [Project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/).

## Version and former Outset projects

`rivet --version` prints the plugin/CLI version. If another installed tool is also named `rivet`, call this checkout's `bin/rivet` explicitly.

```sh
rivet migrate --root /path/to/project --preview
```

Repeat without `--preview` to migrate an Outset project; changed originals are backed up in `.rivet-recovery/`. The editor equivalent is `:RivetMigrate`. Application and module names stay the same. Configure again afterward.

Projects with `.outset.json` require migration before tasks or mutations. The deprecated `outset` launcher delegates to Rivet and honors `OUTSET_LUA`; its warning announces removal in 0.3.0.
